    {
     PaneBox[
      StyleBox["\<\"Figure 5. Top-down view of the same scene.  Same metric, \
same bubble velocity, same spacecraft, but no z-axis cosmetics so the \
geometry is unobstructed.  The red ring is the actual analytic \[Rho] (more \
red = more exotic matter required); the cyan grid is the coordinate mesh \
advected by the shift field; the dashed white circle is the nominal bubble \
radius.\"\>",
       StripOnInput->False,
       FontFamily->"Helvetica",
       FontSize->10,
       FontSlant->Italic],
      Alignment->Center,
      ImageSize->720]}
   },
   DefaultBaseStyle->"Column",
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Center}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{0.6}}}],
  "Column"]], "Output",
 CellMargins->{{20, 20}, {10, 14}},
 ShowCellLabel->False,
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"8f54173a-47c6-41ce-aee0-9066f8ee5760"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Both animations are rendered entirely from the analytic Alcubierre formulas \
\[LongDash] no GR solver, no NDSolve, just the closed forms for the shape \
function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"f", "(", 
    SubscriptBox["r", "s"], ")"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "970a6cfd-6e0f-47d4-947d-07db6b984dba"],
 ", the shift ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["\[Beta]", "x"], "=", "-", 
    SubscriptBox["v", "s"], " ", 
    RowBox[{"f", "(", 
     SubscriptBox["r", "s"], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "f912e641-d22f-429c-b036-997775f2629f"],
 ", and the Eulerian energy density.  Both run in seconds.  The spacecraft \
mesh and animation logic add fewer than 200 lines of Wolfram on top of the \
existing package."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4dd9c4c6-f43b-46bd-b28f-5bab46709b63"],

Cell["6. The grid being warped", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"d4d8413c-ccac-4de2-926d-e337c512642b"],

Cell["\<\
Another way to see what the bubble is doing to the geometry is to drop a \
regular Cartesian grid into the spacetime and let the shift field advect \
every grid intersection.  As the bubble sweeps from left to right across the \
frame the grid lines locally bunch up in front of it and stretch out behind \
it.  The animation below uses a 14\[Times]14 grid, the analytic Alcubierre \
shift field with v=c and R=1, and a simple Euler advection step:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"24677d63-b1be-464f-9134-8a74644a7cc4"],

Cell["\<\
alcShift[t_, x_, y_] := -1.0 TanhShape[Sqrt[(x - 1.0 t)^2 + y^2], 1, 8];

advect[{x0_, y0_}, t_, nstep_] := Module[{x = x0, y = y0, dt = t/nstep},
  Do[{x, y} = {x + dt alcShift[k dt, x, y], y}, {k, 0, nstep - 1}];
  {x, y}];

frame[t_] := Show[
  DensityPlot[Abs[alcShift[t, x, y]], {x, -3, 3}, {y, -2.5, 2.5},
    ColorFunction -> \"SunsetColors\", Frame -> True],
  Graphics[{Thickness[0.0015], Black,
    Table[Line[Table[advect[{x, yi}, t, 14], {x, -3, 3, 6/60}]],
          {yi, -3, 3, 6/14}],
    Table[Line[Table[advect[{xi, y}, t, 14], {y, -3, 3, 6/60}]],
          {xi, -3, 3, 6/14}]}]];
ListAnimate[Table[frame[t], {t, 0, 2, 2/29.}]]\
\>", "Input",
 CellTags->"runnable",ExpressionUUID->"ba9c3259-b60b-40ef-8ebe-2f43fd1a1b44"],

