Cell["9. Why this is hard: energy conditions", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"369406d0-5042-485d-b2ab-fc38d9f8148b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The classical energy conditions are coordinate-invariant statements about \
which kinds of stress-energy are \"reasonable\".  Four of them are standard.  \
The ",
 StyleBox["weak energy condition (WEC)",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " says any timelike observer measures non-negative energy density,"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d6f7e9f3-d307-4f18-b333-5455bbc828f7"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox["T", 
    RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "\[Nu]"}]], " ", 
   SuperscriptBox["u", "\[Mu]"], " ", 
   SuperscriptBox["u", "\[Nu]"], "\[GreaterEqual]", "0", "\[ThickSpace]\[ThickSpace]\[ThickSpace]", 
   RowBox[{"\[ForAll]", " ", "timelike", " ", "u"}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula",
 TextAlignment->Center,
 FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->"7ad5b636-7498-470f-981e-679c393a4cf5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox["null energy condition (NEC)",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " is the same statement specialised to null observers (limiting behaviour as \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"v", "\[Rule]", "c"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "9d0a0c78-8026-4f77-a120-911545e679eb"],
 "):"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7137278c-c42b-446e-80df-df2318d1965b"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox["T", 
    RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "\[Nu]"}]], " ", 
   SuperscriptBox["k", "\[Mu]"], " ", 
   SuperscriptBox["k", "\[Nu]"], "\[GreaterEqual]", "0", "\[ThickSpace]\[ThickSpace]\[ThickSpace]", 
   RowBox[{"\[ForAll]", " ", "null", " ", "k"}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula",
 TextAlignment->Center,
 FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->"e8b84a85-7f45-42a5-a774-505a4b27d0f1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox["strong energy condition (SEC)",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " controls the trace-reversed combination,"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7ddaac89-f3ae-4ccb-9179-bcf3aaafbb4f"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["T", 
      RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "\[Nu]"}]], "-", 
     RowBox[{
      FractionBox["1", "2"], " ", "T", " ", 
      SubscriptBox["g", 
       RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "\[Nu]"}]]}]}], ")"}], " ", 
   SuperscriptBox["u", "\[Mu]"], " ", 
   SuperscriptBox["u", "\[Nu]"], "\[GreaterEqual]", "0"}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula",
 TextAlignment->Center,
 FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->"7523e9ee-fe86-463d-b43a-e634d27c41df"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"T", "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["g", 
      RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "\[Nu]"}]], " ", 
     SubscriptBox["T", 
      RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "\[Nu]"}]]}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "c41b2dfb-b29a-4b80-aa89-fffe6a4a012c"],
 " the trace of the stress-energy.  The SEC is what enforces gravitational \
attraction (the focusing theorem); cosmologists routinely violate it with \
positive cosmological constant or inflation.  Finally the ",
 StyleBox["dominant energy condition (DEC)",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " is the statement that energy never propagates faster than light: WEC plus \
the requirement that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"-", 
    SubsuperscriptBox["T", "\[Nu]", "\[Mu]"], " ", 
    SuperscriptBox["u", "\[Nu]"]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "8ccfde88-04b6-4964-abac-52231db15972"],
 " be a non-spacelike (timelike or null) 4-vector for every timelike ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["u", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "14fce4a3-0c8c-40e6-89ca-6fb7b870892c"],
 "."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"adfe10f6-37e3-484d-a74a-e6062bafaf07"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For ordinary matter \[LongDash] dust, photons, fluids, electromagnetic \
fields \[LongDash] all four are obviously satisfied.  For an Alcubierre \
bubble at superluminal speed all four are ",
 StyleBox["violated",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", which is the rigorous statement of \"requires exotic matter\".  The \
package ships sampling-based checkers for all four conditions; applied to a \
wall point of the v=c Alcubierre bubble (the option ",
 StyleBox["EvalAt", "Input"],
 " substitutes coordinates after symbolic differentiation so the random \
observer sampler operates on a numerical metric):"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"893d53b7-a093-4a3d-91ab-f685a01e2da1"],

Cell["\<\
SeedRandom[42];
g = AlcubierreMetric[{1.0, 1.0, 8.0}, {t, x, y, z}];
CheckAllConditions[g, {t, x, y, z},
   EvalAt -> {t -> 0., x -> 1.0, y -> 0.2, z -> 0.}]
(*
  <| WEC -> {False, -0.288},
     NEC -> {False, -0.480},
     SEC -> {False, -0.488},
     DEC -> {False, -Infinity} |>
  All four classical conditions are violated.  DEC reports
  -Infinity because CheckDEC short-circuits when WEC has already
  failed (a non-spacelike energy current presupposes non-negative
  energy density).  This is what \"exotic matter\" means in cold
  equation form.
*)\
\>", "Input",
 CellTags->"runnable",ExpressionUUID->"46eb22a2-5eda-4086-83e8-629ba0bed0b7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Bobrick & Martire's 2021 paper showed something striking about this \
picture: drop the requirement of superluminal speed and the energy conditions \
can be respected.  Subluminal positive-energy warp drives ",
 StyleBox["exist",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ".  They still cost an enormous amount of energy (orders of magnitude above \
any human energy budget), but the matter content is in principle sourceable \
by ordinary fields:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"66269835-a0c1-4445-a8f4-d18e3f305fa1"],

Cell["\<\
SeedRandom[42];
gBM = BobrickMartireMetric[{0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 0.1, 5.0},
                            {t, x, y, z}];   (* v=0.5c, shell *)
CheckAllConditions[gBM, {t, x, y, z},
   EvalAt -> {t -> 0., x -> 1.5, y -> 0., z -> 0.}]   (* in the shell *)
(*
  <| WEC -> {True,  +0.0032},
     NEC -> {True,  +0.00030},
     SEC -> {False, -0.00136},
     DEC -> {True,  -1.16e-5} |>
  WEC, NEC, and DEC are satisfied -- positive-energy matter could
  in principle source this geometry.  SEC is violated, but SEC is
  the condition that enforces gravitational attraction; positive
  cosmological constant and inflation violate it routinely, so an
  SEC-only violation is qualitatively different from the Alcubierre
  case where every observer-independent condition fails.
*)\
\>", "Input",
 CellTags->"runnable",ExpressionUUID->"43a3b265-a163-4cb8-8b8c-6aaf7425d1ad"],

Cell["\<\
This is the clearest path forward in the literature: instead of trying to \
engineer exotic matter, find geometries which require none, even if the \
resulting drive is slower-than-light.  None of this rescues warp drives from \
being hypothetical objects \[LongDash] even subluminal positive-energy \
constructions demand stress-energy distributions far outside any matter \
humans know how to assemble, and the energy budgets remain many orders of \
magnitude beyond global energy production.  The value of writing the metrics \
down is not that they are buildable, but that they sharpen what general \
relativity does and does not forbid: causality and the field equations alone \
do not rule out faster-than-light effective travel; what rules it out, in \
practice, is the matter sector.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f6ce6117-f8c9-4692-a2d4-f36c49af9208"],

Cell["10. Implementation notes", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"603876e7-e16e-42ff-a0e3-6288a4d4eeba"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] The Wolfram port is a side-by-side translation of a \
~5\[ThinSpace]900-line Python package.  Wolfram's symbolic ",
 StyleBox["D[\[CenterEllipsis]]", "Input"],
 " replaces the Python finite-difference Christoffel/Riemann/Ricci loops, \
which compresses the GR module from ~2\[ThinSpace]100 lines to ~140 and \
removes a step-size hyperparameter.\n",
 "\[Bullet] Each metric is exposed as a function ",
 StyleBox["<Name>Metric[params, coords]", "Input"],
 " that returns a 4\[Times]4 symbolic ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["g", 
    RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "\[Nu]"}]], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "a088a2ef-d618-432c-8603-881d56a94ed4"],
 ".  Mix and match: pass any of them through ",
 StyleBox["EinsteinTensor", "Input"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["KretschmannScalar", "Input"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["IntegrateGeodesic", "Input"],
 ", or ",
 StyleBox["CheckAllConditions", "Input"],
 ".\n",
 "\[Bullet] Geodesics use ",
 StyleBox["NDSolve", "Input"],
 " against a precomputed Christoffel-symbol expression.  The right-hand side \
is built once as a Wolfram pure ",
 StyleBox["Function", "Input"],
 " (closed-form symbolic, not byte-compiled by ",
 StyleBox["Compile", "Input"],
 ") so each ",
 StyleBox["NDSolve", "Input"],
 " step substitutes numerical values into the same expression without \
re-running the symbolic differentiation \[LongDash] no per-step finite \
differencing.\n",
 "\[Bullet] All animations in this notebook are pure Wolfram (no Python).  \
They use the analytic Alcubierre formulas where possible, and a simple Euler \
advection of the shift field for the geodesic sweep.\n",
 "\[Bullet] Tests in ",
 StyleBox["Wolfram/Tests.wls", "Input"],
 " verify the Minkowski limit, the unit determinant of the Alcubierre metric, \
the ADM round-trip, and that flat-space Christoffel symbols vanish."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"145dec1c-5bba-4188-8178-b3165033d096"],

Cell["11. What I take away", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"6faf591b-1b4e-414d-a1e9-32d91c6f7492"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Symbolic GR is enormously practical in Wolfram.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " The whole tensor calculus chain \[LongDash] metric, Christoffel, Riemann, \
Ricci, Einstein, Weyl, Kretschmann \[LongDash] fits in 140 lines of Wolfram \
against ~1\[ThinSpace]100 lines of NumPy with finite differences.  The \
symbolic answer is exact, valid at every point of spacetime simultaneously, \
and trivially substituted into for numerical work.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Exotic matter is real, mathematically.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " The Alcubierre energy density is closed-form negative; the classical \
energy conditions are violated unambiguously.  Whether that exotic matter is \
physically realisable is an open question \[LongDash] Casimir-effect \
calculations and squeezed-light experiments do produce regions of locally \
negative energy density, but at densities and volumes ~30 orders of magnitude \
below what a useful warp drive needs.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Subluminal drives are positive-energy.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " Bobrick\[Dash]Martire's 2021 result is the most encouraging recent \
development: the ban on warp drives only applies above ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["v", "s"], "=", "c"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "16799fa2-f7ca-48ae-a28e-ed432e081adf"],
 ".  Below it, perfectly normal matter can in principle do the job.  The \
price is enormous \[LongDash] still much more than any existing energy source \
\[LongDash] but it is finite, positive, and not magic.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Wolfram is a great teaching tool here.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " A full pipeline (define metric, compute Einstein tensor, plot energy \
density, integrate geodesic, animate) fits on a single laptop screen.  The \
same student who would struggle to set up Christoffel symbols on paper can \
produce Figure 1 in two minutes in a notebook.  This is the workflow GR \
education ought to be using by default."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"32c63ba2-58e8-4db2-9053-c48697d4dd2d"],

